This file contains our Django models.
The responsibility of a model is to store common fields that most clients will need.
We follow the SRP and avoid adding fields that are only relevant to certain clients.
A model may have property functions to give access to derived data.
Our models inherit from BaseModel, which supplies the id field and some timestamp fields.

Example.

```python
class Plot(BaseModel):
    # We use TextField instead of CharField
    name = models.TextField()
    # Polygons are stored in a PolygonField
    polygon = models.PolygonField()
    # Usually foreign keys use CASCADE, but you are free to choose the most appropriate option
    project = models.ForeignKey(Project, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name="plots")
    organization = models.ForeignKey(Organization, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name="plots")
    # Here is an example of a choice field that is based on an Enum
    land_type = models.TextField(choices=PlotType.choices, default=PlotType.UNDEFINED)
    # Here is an example of a multiple-choice field that is based on an Enum
    crops = UniqueArrayField(models.TextField(choices=Crop.choices), blank=True, default=list)
    # A Meta class may be used to define constraints
    class Meta:
        constraints = [
            models.UniqueConstraint(fields=["name", "organization"], name="unique_name_per_org"),
        ]
    # We use __str__ to define a human-readable representation of the object
    def __str__(self):
        return self.name
```

